<h2>The statutory design at work</h2>
<p>The 2015 amendments rebuilt the relationship between courts and tribunals on interim relief. Section 9(2) prevents a party from obtaining pre-arbitration protection and then sitting on it: the measure is conditioned on actually starting the arbitration, within ninety days or the period the court fixes. Section 9(3) completes the design by making the tribunal, once constituted, the default forum for interim measures under Section 17 — court intervention thereafter requires showing that the tribunal&rsquo;s remedy would be inefficacious. Section 17, in turn, gives the tribunal the same powers as a court under Section 9, with its orders enforceable as orders of the court. The present order compresses that entire architecture into a single disposal: protection now, arbitration promptly, tribunal thereafter.</p>

<h2>Why courts prefer this structure</h2>
<p>Disposing of a Section 9 petition with a commencement condition, rather than keeping it alive for months of affidavits, serves both sides. The applicant gets immediate protection — often the only thing that genuinely cannot wait. The respondent gets a guarantee that the protection cannot become a standing attachment divorced from any adjudication: if the applicant does not arbitrate, the relief dies of its own terms; if it does, the respondent&rsquo;s objections on maintainability, jurisdiction and merits travel intact to the tribunal, which can modify or vacate the arrangement under Section 17. The court, for its part, avoids becoming the de facto case-management forum for a dispute the parties contracted to resolve elsewhere.</p>
<table class="law">
