  <tr><td>Before arbitration commences</td><td>Court</td><td>Section 9(1), disciplined by Section 9(2)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Tribunal constituted, arbitration pending</td><td>Arbitral Tribunal (court only if S.17 relief inefficacious)</td><td>Sections 9(3) and 17</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Post-award, pre-enforcement</td><td>Court</td><td>Section 9(1)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Diarise the fuse.</strong> A commencement condition is self-executing. Issue the Section 21 notice invoking arbitration well inside the window, and keep proof of service — commencement dates from the respondent&rsquo;s receipt of the request.</p>
  <p><strong>For respondents, aim at Section 17.</strong> Where the court has left contentions open, the efficient battlefield is the tribunal&rsquo;s first interim hearing, not a review of the Section 9 disposal.</p>
  <p><strong>Match the relief to preservation.</strong> Courts grant bridge relief readily when it preserves the status quo — assets, accounts, goods — and sparingly when it would amount to final relief in interim dress.</p>
</div>
